# App secrets ÔÇö Next.js reads THIS file at runtime. Copy to .env.local (local dev) and
# set the same values in Vercel ÔåÆ Project ÔåÆ Settings ÔåÆ Environment Variables (production).

# Secret used to sign session tokens (HMAC). Use a long random string, e.g.:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# Rotating it logs everyone out.
AUTH_SECRET="generate-a-long-random-string"

# Invite code required to register a new account. Share it with people you
# want on the app; rotate it to stop new sign-ups.
INVITE_CODE="pick-an-invite-code"

# Shared secret for the scheduled sync at /api/cron/<job>. Generate it the same
# way as AUTH_SECRET. Set it in Vercel and the crons in vercel.json start
# working; leave it unset and the endpoint returns 503, so nobody can trigger
# third-party API spend on your key.
#
#   /api/cron/grade    settle finished events from ESPN scores (free)
#   /api/cron/closing  closing odds near kickoff via The Odds API (same as Synka)
#   /api/cron/clv      near-kickoff closing capture via The Odds API
#
# vercel.json schedules the first two daily, which is all a Vercel Hobby plan
# allows (2 crons, once per day). Retroactive closing odds come from the local
# OddsPortal scrape (npm run scrape:clv).
#
# /api/cron/clv is deliberately NOT scheduled there: it only captures bets whose
# kickoff is inside a T-30…T+5 window, so it has to run every ~10 minutes to be
# worth anything. On a Pro plan add it to vercel.json with "*/10 * * * *"; on
# Hobby, point an external scheduler (cron-job.org and friends) at
#   https://<your-app>/api/cron/clv?key=<CRON_SECRET>
# every 10 minutes. Either way it replaces the Windows scheduled task, which
# only ran while that one machine was powered on and logged in.
# CRON_SECRET="generate-a-long-random-string"

# Optional — The Odds API key.
# Powers event search + auto-grading, and fills the /ev odds screen with the
# Swedish books (Betsson, NordicBet, Coolbet, Betinia, Unibet, LeoVegas,
# Expekt, 888sport, Svenska Spel, ATG, Hajper, CampoBet, Mr Green, BetMGM SE).
# https://the-odds-api.com/
#
# Cost is 1 credit per region AND market. The /ev ingest uses regions=eu,se
# and h2h only: 2 leagues x 1 market x 2 regions = 4 credits per refresh.
# regions MUST include "se" or no Swedish book comes back at all.
# Check what a key returns before ingesting:  npm run odds:probe -- --sports
# ODDS_API_KEY=your_key_here

# Optional ÔÇö separate database for the shot model (/skott). Kept apart from the
# bet journal on purpose: it holds bulk-reloaded reference data (matches, odds,
# model projections) that must never be able to touch your bets.
# Create a second Neon database and connect it in Vercel with Custom Prefix
# "SHOTS" ÔÇö the integration then creates both names below automatically.
# Locally, paste them into .env.local and run:
#   npm run db:push:shots
# The endpoint MUST differ from DATABASE_URL above; same endpoint = same
# database as your bets, which defeats the point of the split.
# SHOTS_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://...@ep-xxx-pooler.../neondb?sslmode=require"
# SHOTS_DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED="postgresql://...@ep-xxx.../neondb?sslmode=require"

# Optional — separate database for the CS2 module (/cs2). Same reasoning as the
# shot model: bulk-loaded reference data (teams, players, maps, demo facts)
# that must never be able to touch your bets. Recommended: its own Neon
# database (Vercel → Storage → Create Database → Neon, Custom Prefix "CS2"
# creates both names below). Alternative: the SAME endpoint as
# SHOTS_DATABASE_URL with its own Postgres schema — append `&schema=cs2`.
# Never point it at DATABASE_URL or SHOTS_DATABASE_URL without `schema=cs2` —
# `npm run db:push:cs2` drops tables that are not in prisma/cs2.prisma.
#   npm run db:push:cs2
# CS2_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://...@ep-yyy-pooler.../neondb?sslmode=require"
# CS2_DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED="postgresql://...@ep-yyy.../neondb?sslmode=require"
#
# HLTV scraping (local only — runs a real Chromium via Playwright):
# CS2_HLTV_THROTTLE_MS=4000     # min wait between pages; actual wait is 1–2×
# CS2_HLTV_MAX_PAGES=1500       # page budget per run (cache hits are free)
# CS2_CACHE_DIR=.cache/cs2      # HTML cache, browser profile, demo facts
# CS2_BROWSER_CHANNEL=msedge    # chrome | msedge | chromium (default: Chrome, then Edge, then bundled Chromium)
# If Cloudflare keeps re-asking, drive your own browser instead. Start Edge with
#   & "C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft\Edge\Application\msedge.exe" --remote-debugging-port=9222 --user-data-dir=C:\cs2-edge
# open hltv.org once in it, then set:
# CS2_CDP_URL=http://localhost:9222
#
# Fetch HLTV pages through Scrapling instead of patchright (same as passing
# --scrapling). Scrapling solves the Cloudflare check by itself, so the window
# can stay hidden. Needs `npm run scrapling:setup` once.
# CS2_FETCHER=scrapling
#
# "Hämta från Unibet" in the CS2 lines panel reads Kambi's public offering JSON
# (unofficial — may change without notice). Another Kambi brand works too:
# CS2_KAMBI_BRAND=ubse          # Kambi brand code in the offering URL
# CS2_KAMBI_BOOKMAKER=Unibet    # bookmaker name saved on the lines

# Optional - Anthropic API key. Powers the "Fran kvitto" button on the Bets page:
# Claude reads a betslip screenshot and prefills the log-bet form (bookmaker, odds,
# stake, market, kickoff). Leave it unset and the button answers that the parser is
# not configured; manual entry is unaffected. Set the same key in Vercel ->
# Settings -> Environment Variables to use it from the phone.
# https://console.anthropic.com/
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...

# Optional - model used for that parsing. Default: claude-haiku-4-5, the cheapest
# one that reads receipts reliably. Switch to claude-sonnet-5 if yours are misread.
# BETSLIP_PARSE_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5

# Optional - Scrapling (Python) fetcher used by the local scrapers. Set up once with
#   npm run scrapling:setup     (creates .venv and downloads its Chromium)
#   npm run scrapling:check     (fetches a harmless page both ways)
# The scripts find .venv by themselves; set these only to override.
# SCRAPLING_PYTHON=C:\Python312\python.exe   # Python that has scrapling installed
# SCRAPLING_BROWSER_PATH=                    # own Chromium binary for the browser mode
#
# Sofascore (shots:ingest:sofascore, props:grade, sofascore:capture) goes through
# the same fetcher. Defaults are deliberately slow; Sofascore blocks cloud/VPN IPs.
# SOFASCORE_THROTTLE_MS=1500    # min wait between requests; actual wait is 1–2×
# SOFASCORE_MAX_REQUESTS=800    # request budget per run (cache hits are free)
# SOFASCORE_CACHE_DIR=.cache/sofascore
